ai_mode.launch_replay = Play replay
ai_mode.replay_hint = Published replays (Elements/Replays), validated automatically on load.
ai_mode.level_tip = <b>The level the AI trains on.</b><br>A model learns one level at a time: it memorises that particular course, not "how to play" in general. To find out whether it understood anything, train here and then evaluate on a different level in the Validation tab.
ai_mode.algo_evo_tip = <b>Evolutionary — the easiest one to get working. Start here.</b><br>A population of players is tried out, the best are kept and copied with small variations. No derivatives are computed, so there is nothing delicate to tune.<br><b>Pick it if:</b> you are new to AI mode, or another algorithm is stalling and you first want to know whether the level is solvable at all.
ai_mode.algo_pg_tip = <b>REINFORCE — the first real gradient-based learner.</b><br>The AI plays a whole game, looks at the score, then nudges its reflexes towards what would have scored better.<br><b>Its weakness:</b> two nearly identical games can score very differently, so the corrections pull in every direction at once. That is exactly what actor-critic fixes.
ai_mode.algo_ac_tip = <b>Actor-critic — REINFORCE, made steadier.</b><br>A second network, the critic, learns to predict the score of a situation. Corrections are no longer based on the raw score but on the gap between the score obtained and the score expected: a far less noisy signal.<br><b>Extra setting:</b> "Critic learning rate".
ai_mode.algo_dqn_tip = <b>DQN — the most powerful, and the easiest to misconfigure.</b><br>The AI rates every possible action in every situation, replays past memories to learn several times from each experience, and explores at random less and less often.<br><b>Before launching it</b>, read the tooltips in the "Advanced DQN" box: three of its eight settings can on their own make learning completely invisible, even while the network is learning perfectly well.
ai_mode.population_tip = <b>How many different players are tried each generation.</b><br>Usual value: <b>32 to 128</b>.<br><b>Too small:</b> every player looks alike after a few generations and the search stalls. <b>Too large:</b> each generation costs proportionally more time — 128 individuals means 128 games played before any progress is shown.
ai_mode.mutation_rate_tip = <b>How often a reflex gets randomly altered in a child.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.02 to 0.10</b>. This setting is the "how often"; mutation strength is the "by how much".<br><b>Too low:</b> children are carbon copies of their parents and the curve is flat. <b>Too high:</b> every child is a stranger, and good discoveries are destroyed as soon as they are made.
ai_mode.episodes_tip = <b>How many games the AI will play in total</b> (REINFORCE, actor-critic, DQN).<br>Usual value: <b>1000 to 10000</b>. Below a few hundred, no gradient algorithm has the material time to learn anything.<br><b>For DQN</b>, this number must be consistent with "Exploration decay steps": it is the run's total step count that decides whether the AI ever gets to stop playing at random.<br>The Evolutionary algorithm ignores this field and uses "Max generations".
ai_mode.learning_rate_tip = <b>How large each correction to the AI's reflexes is.</b><br>The single most sensitive setting. Usual value: <b>0.001 to 0.01</b> for REINFORCE and actor-critic, but <b>0.0001 to 0.0005</b> for DQN, which is markedly more fragile.<br><b>Too low:</b> the curve rises, but so slowly it looks flat. <b>Too high:</b> the curve turns into violent sawteeth, or collapses all at once after a good start — the AI has "forgotten" everything it knew.
ai_mode.gamma_tip = <b>How much the AI cares about the future rather than the present.</b><br>At 0.90 it sees only a handful of moves ahead; at 0.99 it plans far. Usual value: <b>0.95 to 0.99</b>.<br><b>Too low:</b> the AI lunges at the nearest immediate reward and ignores a detour that would pay more.<br><b>Careful with DQN: never set 1.00.</b> The AI then sums its future gains without ever discounting them, its estimates grow without bound, and learning diverges instead of converging.
ai_mode.optimizer_tip = <b>The method used to apply corrections.</b><br><b>adam</b> tunes the step size per neuron on its own: the default, and the right answer in almost every case.<br><b>sgd</b> applies the raw learning rate with no adjustment. More predictable, but it demands a far more carefully tuned learning rate.
ai_mode.seed_tip = <b>The starting point of randomness.</b><br>Same seed and same settings = a strictly identical run, down to the decimal.<br><b>Tip:</b> before concluding that one setting beats another, re-run it on two or three different seeds — on a single run you are mostly measuring luck.
ai_mode.launch_training_tip = <b>Starts training in the background</b>; the window stays usable and the curve fills in as games are played.
ai_mode.stop_training_tip = <b>Stops training at the end of the current game.</b><br>Nothing is lost: the best model so far is already saved in the run folder.
ai_mode.generation_tip = <b>Which training step "Watch in game" will replay.</b><br>Comparing an early step against a late one is the most direct way to see whether the AI actually improved, beyond what the curve claims.
ai_mode.preview_tip_waiting = Available from the run's first preview: training writes one regularly, a few seconds after launch.
ai_mode.preview_tip = <b>Replays the best game of the selected step inside the level.</b><br>A flat curve does not tell you <i>why</i> the AI is failing; watching it play does.
ai_mode.run_tip = <b>The training run to evaluate</b>, read from the runs folder chosen in the Training tab.
ai_mode.repetitions_tip = <b>How many games are replayed to measure the success rate.</b><br>Usual value: <b>30 to 100</b>.<br>Over 5 games, an 80% rate means nothing. The higher the count, the more trustworthy the measurement and the longer the evaluation.
ai_mode.evaluate_tip = <b>Replays the model N times</b> on the chosen level and decoding mode, then computes the statistics.
ai_mode.save_model_tip = <b>Copies the model's weights to a file of your choosing.</b><br>They are saved in the run folder either way.
ai_mode.export_replay_tip = <b>Replays the model deterministically and publishes the resulting sequence</b> to Elements/Replays, where it becomes visible from the Replay tab.<br>Writes nothing if the model does not finish the level.
