ai_mode.group_common = Common
ai_mode.group_evolutionary = Evolutionary
ai_mode.group_gradient = Gradient-based (REINFORCE / Actor-critic / DQN)
ai_mode.group_dqn = DQN advanced
ai_mode.stability = Stable generations
ai_mode.dqn_replay_capacity = Replay memory capacity
ai_mode.dqn_replay_capacity_tip = <b>How many memories the AI keeps to revise from.</b><br>DQN does not learn only from the game in progress: it draws from this memory book at every correction.<br><b>How to choose it:</b> the book should cover <b>several hundred games</b>. A game is a few hundred steps long, so aim for <b>50,000 to 200,000</b>.<br><b>Too small (a few thousand):</b> the book holds only the last few games, the AI revises the same recent memories over and over and overfits to them. A common failure, and a hard one to spot on the curve.
ai_mode.dqn_batch_size = Mini-batch size
ai_mode.dqn_batch_size_tip = <b>How many memories are re-read at each correction.</b><br>Usual value: <b>32 to 128</b>.<br><b>Larger:</b> more reliable corrections, but <b>every game step becomes proportionally slower</b>. Together with the update period, this is the main driver of a DQN run's duration — doubling this number roughly doubles the compute time.
ai_mode.dqn_warmup_size = Transitions before the first update
ai_mode.dqn_warmup_size_tip = <b>How many memories to gather before learning starts at all.</b><br>Usual value: <b>1,000 to 10,000</b>, and at minimum a few times the mini-batch size.<br>It prevents the very first corrections from being computed on a near-empty book, where the same three memories would be re-read on a loop.
ai_mode.dqn_update_period = Update period (steps)
ai_mode.dqn_update_period_tip = <b>How many game steps pass between two corrections.</b><br>Usual value: <b>1 to 4</b>.<br><b>At 1</b> the AI corrects itself at every step: the most learning per game played, and by far the slowest setting. Combined with a large mini-batch it can stretch a run to tens of hours.<br><b>Move to 4</b> to cut compute time fourfold while learning almost as much.
ai_mode.dqn_target_sync_period = Target network sync period (steps)
ai_mode.dqn_target_sync_period_tip = <b>How many steps pass before the AI refreshes its reference point.</b><br>DQN corrects itself by comparing against a frozen copy of itself. If that copy changed constantly, the AI would be chasing its own shadow and would never learn anything stable.<br>Usual value: <b>500 to 5,000</b>.<br><b>Too short:</b> unstable learning, the curve oscillates. <b>Too long:</b> the AI aims at a stale reference and progress becomes very slow.
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_start = Initial exploration (epsilon)
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_start_tip = <b>The share of moves played completely at random at the very start of the run.</b><br>Keep <b>1.00</b>: the AI knows nothing yet, so it may as well discover the level by trial and error rather than repeating an arbitrary preference inherited from its initial weights.<br>This share then falls to "Final exploration", over the span set by "Exploration decay steps".
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_end = Final exploration (epsilon)
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_end_tip = <b>The share of random moves once the exploration phase is over.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.01 to 0.10</b>.<br>It never goes to 0: keeping a pinch of randomness lets the AI discover that a route it had written off is in fact the right one.<br><b>Too high:</b> even when trained, the AI misses its precise jumps because one move in five goes off at random.
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_decay = Exploration decay steps
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_decay_tip = <b>How long the exploration phase lasts, counted in GAME STEPS accumulated across the whole run — not in games.</b><br>The AI starts out playing at random and gradually gets serious; this setting says how long that takes.<br><b>How to choose it:</b> a game is a few hundred steps long. Multiply "Episodes" by that length to get the run's total step count, and enter <b>half</b> of it here. For 2,000 games of 300 steps, i.e. 600,000 steps, set around <b>300,000</b>.<br><b>Too low:</b> the AI stops exploring too early and locks onto the first mediocre strategy it found.<br><b>Too high: this is DQN's most common failure.</b> The run ends before the decay does, the AI is still playing almost entirely at random from start to finish, and its curve stays hopelessly flat — even though the network was learning perfectly well. Watch "Current epsilon": if it has not come down near its final value by halfway through the run, this value is too large.
ai_mode.dqn_epsilon_current = Current epsilon
ai_mode.eval_model = Model
ai_mode.eval_model_tip = <b>The weights file to evaluate.</b><br>Pre-filled from the run chosen above, but a model saved elsewhere can be opened directly.
ai_mode.eval_level = Level
ai_mode.eval_level_tip = <b>The level to measure the model on.</b><br>The same level measures what was learned; <b>a different level</b> measures the ability to generalise — and that is almost always the more instructive measurement.
ai_mode.max_steps = Step budget per episode
ai_mode.max_steps_tip = <b>The maximum length of one game during evaluation.</b><br><b>Leave it on Automatic.</b> A budget set shorter than the training one declares a model "failed" when it does in fact finish the level.
ai_mode.eval_seed = Seed
ai_mode.eval_seed_tip = <b>The evaluation campaign's seed:</b> the same seed replays exactly the same games.<br>Useful for comparing two models under strictly identical conditions.
ai_mode.decoding = Decoding mode
ai_mode.decoding_tip = <b>How the model picks its move during evaluation.</b><br><b>argmax</b> always plays the move it rates highest: a deterministic measurement, and the mode to use when comparing models.<br><b>stochastic</b> draws at random according to its preferences: it measures the behaviour actually seen during training, and usually scores slightly lower.
ai_mode.stop_evaluation = Cancel evaluation
ai_mode.stop_evaluation_tip = <b>Interrupts the campaign at the end of the current game</b>; repetitions already played remain measured.
ai_mode.mean_steps_all = Mean steps (all)
ai_mode.repetitions_run = Repetitions played
ai_mode.export_report = Export CSV report…
ai_mode.export_report_tip = <b>Writes the evaluation result to a CSV file</b>, in the same format as the command line's --report option.
ai_mode.export_report_title = Export the evaluation report
ai_mode.csv_filter = CSV report (*.csv)
ai_mode.report_exported = Report exported.
ai_mode.report_export_failed = Could not export the report.
ai_mode.reuse_run_settings = Reuse this run's settings
ai_mode.reuse_run_settings_tip = <b>Reloads the chosen run's exact settings into the Training tab.</b><br>This is how you make progress: take a run, change <b>one single</b> setting, relaunch, compare.
ai_mode.browse_model_title = Choose a trained model
ai_mode.model_filter = Trained model (*.bin)
ai_mode.export_replay_title = Export the replay
ai_mode.replay_filter = Replay (*.json)
ai_mode.export_not_solved = This model does not finish this level: no replay was written.
ai_mode.hidden_size = Hidden layer size
ai_mode.hidden_size_tip = <b>The "brain size": how many neurons the inner layer has.</b><br>Usual value: <b>32 to 128</b>. 64 is enough for every level in the game.<br><b>Too small:</b> the AI plateaus, unable to tell two similar situations apart. <b>Too large:</b> every game gets noticeably slower without playing any better.<br>A saved model can only be reloaded at the exact size that produced it.
ai_mode.runs_root = Runs folder
ai_mode.runs_root_tip = <b>Where training runs are written:</b> the trained model, the CSV statistics, and the config.json that replays the run exactly.<br>Defaults to the TrainingRuns folder next to the game. Each run gets its own dated subfolder.
ai_mode.browse = Browse…
ai_mode.tournament_size = Tournament size
ai_mode.tournament_size_tip = <b>How many candidates compete to become a parent.</b><br>Usual value: <b>3 to 5</b> for a population of 32.<br><b>Too small (2):</b> poor players reproduce almost as often as good ones and progress drags. <b>Too large:</b> only the very best reproduce, the population turns uniform and locks onto the first strategy found.
ai_mode.mutation_strength = Mutation strength
ai_mode.mutation_strength_tip = <b>By how much a reflex is altered when it is.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.05 to 0.30</b>.<br><b>Too weak:</b> children barely explore around their parents. <b>Too strong:</b> a single mutation wipes out what the parent knew, and the best score wanders up and down with no overall direction.
ai_mode.max_generations = Max generations
ai_mode.max_generations_tip = <b>The ceiling for the Evolutionary algorithm: training stops there, result or not.</b><br>Usual value: <b>200 to 1000</b>. Read the run's length as "population × generations" games played.<br>This setting does not apply to the gradient algorithms, which use "Episodes".
ai_mode.required_successes = Stable successes required
ai_mode.required_successes_tip = <b>How many generations in a row the best player must finish the level for training to be declared a success.</b><br>Usual value: <b>3 to 10</b>.<br>It exists so you do not stop on a fluke: succeeding once can be an accident, succeeding five times running cannot.
ai_mode.load_config = Load a configuration…
ai_mode.load_config_tip = <b>Fills every setting from a JSON configuration file</b> — typically a past run's config.json, to relaunch it identically.
ai_mode.save_config = Save the configuration…
ai_mode.save_config_tip = <b>Writes the current settings to a JSON file</b>, reusable both here and on the command line (--config).
ai_mode.reset_defaults = Reset to defaults
ai_mode.reset_defaults_tip = <b>Returns every setting to the game's defaults.</b><br>The right reflex when a run behaves incomprehensibly: go back to defaults, then change one single setting at a time.
ai_mode.load_config_title = Load a training configuration
ai_mode.save_config_title = Save the training configuration
ai_mode.config_filter = JSON configuration (*.json)
ai_mode.config_saved = Configuration saved.
ai_mode.config_save_failed = Could not save the configuration.
ai_mode.config_load_failed = Configuration file missing or unreadable: settings left unchanged.
ai_mode.chart_empty = Not enough data yet.
ai_mode.chart_moving_average = Moving average
ai_mode.critic_learning_rate = Critic learning rate
ai_mode.critic_learning_rate_tip = <b>The learning rate of the second network, the critic</b> (actor-critic only).<br>Deliberately <b>much higher</b> than the policy's, often by 50 to 100 times: the critic must estimate a score worth tens of points, whereas the policy only has to rank preferences close to 1.<br><b>Too low:</b> the critic lags behind, its predictions are wrong, and the actor is corrected against nothing.
ai_mode.crossover_rate = Crossover rate
ai_mode.crossover_rate_tip = <b>How often a child blends two parents instead of faithfully copying one.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.5 to 0.8</b>.<br>Faithful copies matter: they let a discovery survive intact across several generations. At 1.00, nothing is ever passed on unchanged.
ai_mode.batch_episodes = Episodes per batch
ai_mode.batch_episodes_tip = <b>How many games are played before each correction.</b><br>Usual value: <b>1 to 8</b>.<br><b>Larger:</b> each correction is better founded, being averaged over several games — but you get proportionally fewer of them for the same compute time.<br>Raise it if the curve is very noisy; lower it if progress is too slow.
ai_mode.entropy = Entropy
ai_mode.entropy_tip = <b>A bonus paid to the AI for staying undecided.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.005 to 0.02</b>.<br>Without it the AI freezes: it replays the identical game every time, stops trying anything new, and its curve goes permanently flat. <b>Too high:</b> the opposite — it is paid to hesitate and never commits.
ai_mode.exploration_floor = Minimum exploration
ai_mode.exploration_floor_tip = <b>The share of moves drawn completely at random, whatever the AI prefers.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.02 to 0.10</b>. It is entropy's safety net: it guarantees no move ever becomes literally impossible to retry.<br><b>Too high:</b> the AI is constantly disrupted by random moves and can no longer chain a precise sequence, such as a pixel-perfect jump.
ai_mode.gradient_clip = Gradient clipping
ai_mode.gradient_clip_tip = <b>A ceiling on how large one correction may be.</b><br>Usual value: <b>0.5 to 5</b>. A value of 0 disables the ceiling.<br>It stops one freak game — an exceptionally bad score — from destroying everything the AI had learned in a single step.<br>If your curve collapses abruptly now and then, lower this value.
ai_mode.action_repeat = Frames per decision
ai_mode.action_repeat_tip = <b>How many game frames one decision is held for.</b><br>Usual value: <b>2 to 8</b>.<br><b>At 1, the AI does not move:</b> it changes its mind 60 times a second, the random directions cancel out, and the character shivers on the spot.<br><b>Too high:</b> the AI can no longer react in time to a precise jump or an incoming obstacle.
ai_mode.step_budget = Step budget
ai_mode.step_budget_tip = <b>The maximum length of one game, in frames.</b><br>Past this budget the game is stopped and counted as a failure.<br><b>Leave it on Automatic:</b> it is then derived from the level's actual route, mechanisms included. A manual value set too short fails an AI that was playing correctly, just slowly.
ai_mode.stuck_threshold = Stuck threshold
ai_mode.stuck_threshold_tip = <b>How many frames without any progress before a game is abandoned.</b><br>Stops the whole budget being wasted on an AI wedged against a wall.<br><b>Leave it on Automatic</b> (derived from the step budget). <b>Too low:</b> games get cut during legitimate waits, such as watching for a moving platform.
